      "caveats": "The empirical findings and synthesized indices are limited to the state of South Carolina.",
      "what": "The study applied Iterative Proportional Updating (IPU) to harmonize survey and census data in South Carolina, synthesizing population-level indices of perceived transportation barriers at the census tract level. Regression analysis revealed that socioeconomic vulnerabilities are more strongly associated with perceived financial, time, and safety barriers to healthcare than objective spatial proximity. Notably, urban residents reported higher financial and time burdens than rural residents, and higher pedestrian network density in minority-concentrated tracts correlated with lower perceived safety.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that objective infrastructure metrics, like pedestrian network density or spatial proximity, fail to capture actual healthcare accessibility barriers, proving that infrastructure quantity cannot substitute for environmental quality and safety."
